.Dd August 17, 2026
.Dt VV 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm vv
.Nd print a tool version as a strict SemVer triple
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Ar tool
.Pp
.Nm
.Fl -verified-version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
prints exactly one
.Li X.Y.Z
line for
.Ar tool .
Missing or unparseable versions produce
.Li 0.0.0 .
Normal lookups always exit zero.
.Pp
Lookup first reads a declared version file at
.Pa <prefix>/share/vv/<tool>
without executing the tool, then tries the strict
.Fl -verified-version
protocol, then a tool-agnostic ladder of common version flags
.Pq Fl -version , Fl version , Cm version , Fl V , Fl v .
The first source that yields a dotted numeric version wins. Any other result is
.Li 0.0.0 .
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl -verified-version , Fl -version
Print the
.Nm
version.
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print brief help.
.El
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width VV_TIMEOUT
.It Ev VV_TIMEOUT
Probe timeout in seconds. Default: 5.
.El
.Sh COMPATIBILITY
.Nm
keeps no per-command exceptions: every tool is tried the same way. A tool
becomes fully compatible by shipping a
.Pa share/vv/<tool>
version file or by implementing
.Fl -verified-version ;
otherwise the generic flag ladder is attempted. Only a dotted numeric version is
accepted, so a bare integer in help text is never mistaken for a version. A tool
that reports no parseable version resolves to
.Li 0.0.0 .
There are no user recipe or parser configuration files.
.Pp
A tool that resolves to a path inside a macOS application bundle
.Pq Pa *.app
is never executed, because even a bundled command-line stub may open a window on
an unknown option. Such a tool yields
.Li 0.0.0
unless it ships a
.Pa share/vv/<tool>
file, which is read without execution.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
The utility exits zero for every normal result, including missing or
unparseable tools. Invalid options exit two. Any other non-zero status
indicates a defect in
.Nm .
